nd and as weird as it probably  looks and sounds i do a lot of  pretending i pretend as i'm talking to  my audience while i'm folding the  laundry washing the dishes or while i'm  on my way to work i tell you about my  journey to work and that cone that  jumped out at me it was the cone not me  i stand by that it was the cone  so basically i do a lot of talking to  myself and also one of the things that  i've practiced a lot is hand gestures  and what do you do with these things and  where do you put them because for  somebody who had struggled with social  anxiety for years body language did  definitely not come natural to me if you  feel uncomfortable in front of the  camera i highly recommend talking to  yourself in front of the mirror or even  just pretending like you're vlogging  even at the grocery store talk about how  you're gonna pick the best cantaloupe  and even better if you can record  yourself saying random things but you  know just practice vanessa van edwards  and her content on body language has  been a huge help to me so i highly  recommend that you check out her youtube  videos or her book captivate if you feel  like you need some help on body language  whether on or off camera because i think  that it definitely makes other people  feel more comfortable when you feel  comfortable with your own body language  but another way that picturing a friend  helps me is that it keeps into  perspective the what and why i'm doing  what i'm doing because even though i  don't post on a weekly basis like the  youtube gurus say that i should as a mom  as a business owner and as a nurse who  often ends up working overtime i  sacrifice things to be able to post  videos and sometimes  i just want to sleep in and when i get a  comment that a video was helpful or i  gained a new subscriber i remember that  i have a duty to fulfill and that i owe  it to my subscribers so i've said this  before  have a clearly defined why before we go  on leave a camera emoji in the comments  if you're starting your first youtube  channel this year yay  high five to you you got this and  secondly  just start just post if you haven't  started because you're like me and your  schedule is chaotic do what you can but  make it a scheduled date and have  specific times when you must post  undoubtedly your channel will grow much  faster if you do keep a consistent  schedule but if weekly is not doable for  you do what you can but don't let that  be an excuse not to start although it's  not so glamorous growing slowly is  better than never getting started or  dropping it all together  dealing with criticism as a beginner on  youtube  i really want to address criticism  because i feel like in the beginning you  may already have some doubts and so it  can be really easy to let hateful  comments bring you down so i want to  share with you some of the things that  worked for me so that criticism doesn't  stop you from doing the thing that you  want to do there have definitely been  times when i have felt doubt or felt  hurt after reading some of the comments  that are more destructive than  constructive in nature and in those  instances what i do is this little  exercise it sounds crazy but it works i  picture myself as a boat on the ocean  with no other boats around except maybe  you're not a boat you're a yacht yeah  big shiny yacht so picture this with me  you're on the water no other boats  around and when i feel those moments of  doubt creeping up i make this mental  picture in my mind and i tell myself  these words out loud if i am causing the  water to move then i am creating change  it means that i am moving and i repeat  this to myself out loud so that my brain  hears it loud and clear because what is  happening when a boat is on the water  and the water is staying still it's not  moving it's not going to any destination  it's not creating change if you will and  this is the same thing that happens when  you create change it creates waves it  ruffles people's feathers change makes  us uncomfortable it's outside our  comfort zone and our brains perceive it  as danger we're just wired that way so  when we watch people do something that  makes us feel uncomfortable it's a  natural human tendency for us to want  that to stop except some people just  don't go about sharing that in the  nicest way so here's a big tip that i  have for you if you're starting a  youtube channel or growing a youtube  channel no matter how colorful someone's  language may be in the comments  always give them the benefit of the  doubt and if you are going to respond  always be respectful in your response  because at the end of the day that's  your brand that you're representing i  was reading through the comments on a  diy youtube video and somebody had  commented on a better way to do  something and the youtuber responded  with well it's not like i could have dot  dot dot  i just cringed when i saw that because  first of all i didn't think that this  person meant it in a in a harmful rude  way but even if that had been the case  that's not going to entice people to  subscribe if anything people might  decide to leave because of that  defensive response don't get me wrong  i'm not saying to let people step all  over you but just be really cautious in  the way that you respond to everything  because it says a lot about you and your  brand and it's online forever i talk a  lot more in depth about criticism in my  video the three habits that are ruining  your life so check that one out for more  details on the topic because there's a  lot to be said here most likely you  won't start your channel with a defined  color palette a style of editing and  thumbnail design and searching through  other content creators work can be  really helpful to figure out what about  them you like what about their thumbnail  is catchy or the things that you don't  like and the things that you want to  avoid in your own work so for example i  actually started say